# Codewiki — reading a repository into one document

## Goal

Turn a **source checkout** under `docs/raw/<dir>/` into a single, self-contained
markdown document at `docs/raw/<owner>-<repo>.md` that explains how that system
is built — its architecture, its layers, its state machines, its jargon — so a
later session can answer questions about it **without re-reading the code**.

The format is a published interchange shape, not a private one: a document you
compile here and one exported by an external repo-wiki generator are the same
artifact, linted by the same `csdd codewiki lint` and ingested by the same
**wiki** skill.

**Boundary (do not cross):** you produce a **raw source**, not wiki pages. Turning
it into `docs/wiki/pages/` is the **wiki** skill's Ingest workflow
(`/csdd-wiki-ingest`) — a separate step, run after this one. And `docs/raw/` is a
dropzone: **add exactly one new `.md`**, never modify the checkout, never rewrite
an existing raw file.

## The format contract

`csdd codewiki lint` enforces all of this mechanically. Write to it deliberately,
not by imitation.

- **Provenance header**, line 1, exactly:
  `<!-- csdd-codewiki v1 | <owner>/<repo> | src: docs/raw/<dir> | <RFC3339 UTC> | N sections -->`
  `src:` is what lets the lint resolve your citations without a flag. `N` counts
  **every** section, subsections included.
- **`## Structure`** — the tree of numbered titles, before any body. It is what a
  reader navigates by before paying for a single section.
- **Sections** delimited by `<<< SECTION: N.M Title [slug] >>>`, in tree order.
  The slug is the heading lowercased with every non-alphanumeric run collapsed to
  `-`: `3.1 CLI Layer` → `3-1-cli-layer`. Grep `^<<< SECTION:` lists the document.
- **Citations** are markdown links with an **empty target**: `[path/file.go:12-40]()`.
  The empty parens are load-bearing — they are what distinguishes a citation from
  a mermaid label. Paths are relative to the checkout root. Write this one form:
  the lint also accepts the dialects external generators emit (`[`path:1-9`]()`,
  `[[path:1-9]]()`, `[path:34, 505]()`, and a rangeless `[path]()`), but a
  document you author should be uniform.
- **`<details><summary>Relevant source files</summary>`** at the top of each major
  section, listing the files that section was written from as `- [path](path)`.
- **Every section cites at least one source.** Prose with no citation is an
  unfalsifiable claim about code, and the lint fails it.

## Execution Workflow

### 1. Survey — buy ground truth cheaply

Before writing anything, establish what the system *is*:

- The **dependency manifest** first (`go.mod`, `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`,
  `Cargo.toml`). It names the frameworks, and the frameworks tell you what every
  idiom in the tree means: a TUI framework says the UI is a model-update loop, a
  CLI framework says `main` is a command tree, an ORM says the schema lives in
  the models. Read the manifest and half the file layout explains itself.
- The **entry points**: `main.*`, `cmd/`, `index.*`, `bin/`, the `scripts` block.
- A **directory census** — which directories hold the domain, and how big each is.
- The **README** for intent, and the **CI config** for how it is really built.

Do **not** run `csdd graph` here: the graph indexes *this workspace*, not a
vendored checkout.

### 2. Outline — the Structure tree before any prose

Draft the full tree first and keep it stable; retrofitting sections is how trees
and bodies drift. A spine that generalizes across codebases:

1. **Overview** — what it is, the problem it solves, the core concepts.
2. **Installation and Setup** — how it is obtained and run.
3. **Architecture Overview** — the layers, with one subsection per layer.
4.–N. **One section per real subsystem** — the domain, not the directory listing.
   Subsections only where a subsystem has genuine internal structure.
N+1. **Configuration**, then **Development** (build · release · contributing).
N+2. **Glossary** — the codebase's own jargon, each term with a code pointer.

Six to twelve top-level sections. Fewer means you flattened something real; more
means you turned a file listing into a table of contents.

### 3. Author — read, then write, one section at a time

For each section: **read the files it covers in this session**, then write it.
Never the reverse.

- Open with a paragraph stating what the section covers, and cross-reference
  siblings by number ("for the daemon, see page 7.2").
- A **mermaid diagram** for anything with more than two moving parts — call flow,
  state machine, ownership. Node labels carry **real identifiers** — the type,
  method, or file path as it is spelled in the tree — never prose restatements.
- A **table** for every enum, state set, config key, flag, and keybinding. This is
  the format's highest-value density: it is what makes the document answer
  questions instead of describing them.
- **Cite, don't paste.** More than ~15 lines of quoted code means you should have
  cited a range instead.
- Close each subsection with `Sources: [path:a-b](), [path:c-d]()`.

For a large repo, delegate one section per sub-agent — but give each one the
outline and the citation rules, and lint the assembled document as a whole.

### 4. Verify

`csdd codewiki lint docs/raw/<owner>-<repo>.md` — clean, or every remaining
finding explicitly acknowledged to the human.

### 5. Hand off

Say the document is ready for `/csdd-wiki-ingest`. Do **not** ingest it yourself
in the same breath — Ingest is a judgment step the human may want to scope.

## Gotchas

- **Copy file paths, never retype them.** This is the single most common defect in
  generated code wikis, and it is always a plausible-looking near-miss: a
  camelCase filename cited in snake_case, a name shortened to the thing it
  "should" be called, a directory gaining or losing a leading dot. Every one of
  those is a citation to a file that does not exist.
- **Cite the span you actually read.** `1-200` for a file you sampled is a
  fabricated range even when the file is long enough to hide it.
- **Never invent a path to complete a pattern.** If a layer seems to be missing a
  file, the finding is "the code does not appear to do this" — not a plausible
  filename.
- **The checkout is immutable.** No formatting, no `go mod tidy`, no fixes. If it
  has a `.git/`, leave it.
- **The header's `N sections` must match reality.** Update it last, after the
  document is final.
- **Don't restate the README.** Overview explains what the README cannot: how the
  parts fit. Link to the README and move on.

## Verification Before Reporting

- `csdd codewiki lint <file>` ran this session and reports **no findings**.
- Every section carries at least one citation, and every citation names a file and
  a line range **you opened in this session**.
- The `## Structure` tree and the `<<< SECTION:` delimiters agree exactly.
- Nothing under the checkout changed: `git status` on `docs/raw/` shows one new
  `.md` and nothing else.

## Completion Criteria

- `docs/raw/<owner>-<repo>.md` exists, lints clean, and covers architecture,
  every major subsystem, configuration, development, and a glossary.
- The document stands alone: a reader who never opens the checkout can explain how
  the system is built, and knows exactly where to look to verify any claim.
- The human has been told it is ready to ingest with `/csdd-wiki-ingest`.
